<h2>The marriage and the litigation</h2>
<p>The parties married on 25 November 2007 at Arrah, Bihar, and have two daughters born in 2009 and 2014. The husband alleged that the wife was quarrelsome and abusive, assaulted and scratched him, threatened suicide, pressed him to send his aged father away, and removed jewellery from a joint locker. He treated her departure from the matrimonial home on 4 May 2017 as desertion. The wife's case was the mirror image: that she was assaulted, subjected to dowry demands and pressure for a male child, and pushed out of the house with the children — after which the husband stopped paying rent and household expenses.</p>
<p>An earlier restitution suit had been settled through mediation at Ranchi. The husband's subsequent divorce petition under Sections 13(1)(ia) and 13(1)(ib) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 was dismissed by the Family Court, Ranchi on 2 May 2025, and he carried the matter to the High Court in first appeal under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</p>

<h2>Allegation by allegation</h2>
<p>What distinguishes the judgment is its granular treatment of the cruelty case. The Bench tested each pleaded incident against the evidence actually led:</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>The sleeping-pill allegation:</strong> the claim that the wife's conduct caused a pregnancy loss was unsupported — the husband admitted he had no documentary evidence.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate accommodation:</strong> a demand for a separate residence is not cruelty in itself, particularly where the husband himself arranged the housing.</li>
  <li><strong>Complaints to authorities:</strong> the wife's approaches to the Women's Commission and the police were not shown to be knowingly false, so they could not be treated as cruelty.</li>
  <li><strong>The jewellery claim:</strong> the CCTV evidence was weak and the supporting witness could not confirm the removal.</li>
  <li><strong>Psychiatric treatment:</strong> the fact that the husband underwent treatment did not establish that the wife caused the condition.</li>
  <li><strong>Witness quality:</strong> cross-examination revealed that the husband's witnesses had limited personal knowledge of the disputed incidents.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The Bench applied the familiar line of authority on matrimonial cruelty — <em>Dastane</em>, <em>Shobha Rani</em>, <em>V. Bhagat</em>, <em>Vijaykumar Bhate</em>, <em>Vinita Saxena</em>, <em>Manish Tyagi</em> and <em>Joydeep Majumdar</em> — under which the conduct must be grave and weighty, beyond the ordinary wear and tear of married life. Measured against that standard, nothing survived scrutiny.</p>

<h2>Desertion: separation is not enough</h2>
<p>On Section 13(1)(ib), the court applied <em>Lachman Utamchand Kirpalani v. Meena</em> and <em>Debananda Tamuli v. Kakumoni Kataky</em>: desertion requires the fact of separation, the intention to bring cohabitation permanently to an end, absence of consent, and absence of reasonable cause — subsisting for the statutory two-year period. The record showed a separation embedded in disputes over residence, family responsibilities and money. The husband had stopped paying rent in April 2019 and stopped visiting after the wife's parents moved in. On those facts, the wife's animus to abandon the marriage was not established; the length of the separation could not supply what the evidence did not.</p>

<h2>The perversity threshold in first appeals</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1:</strong> The first appellate court may reappraise the whole record — facts and law — under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interference requires perversity: findings that ignore relevant material, rely on irrelevant material, or defy logic (Arulvelu; Damodar Lal).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3:</strong> A plea that the evidence could be read differently is not perversity — the trial court's view prevails.</div>
</div>
<p>Finding no such defect, the Bench dismissed the appeal and upheld the decree of 2 May 2025.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> For petitioners, the judgment is a drafting and proof discipline: plead fewer incidents and prove them properly — with documents, contemporaneous records and witnesses who actually saw what they describe. For respondents, the cross-examination that exposed the witnesses' lack of personal knowledge did decisive work. And for both sides, a prior restitution proceeding and its mediation record will be read closely when the same spouse later seeks dissolution.</p>
</div>
